{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The brachial plexus is the nerve network that supplies the shoulder girdle and upper limb. It is formed by the ventral rami of C5 to C8 and T1. From proximal to distal it has roots, trunks, divisions, cords and branches. The cords give five terminal nerves: musculocutaneous, axillary, radial, median and ulnar.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="AN10.3, AN10.6" phase="Phase 1" /%}

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/brachial-plexus/brachial-plexus-overview-in-situ.webp" alt="Labelled illustration of the brachial plexus passing from the neck behind the clavicle into the axilla and arm." caption="The brachial plexus runs from the neck, behind the clavicle, into the axilla and then divides into the nerves of the upper limb." credit="Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons" license="Public domain" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brachial_plexus_2.svg" width="960" height="720" /%}

## What is the brachial plexus?

A **plexus** is a network where nerves exchange fibres before they continue as new nerves. The brachial plexus carries the motor and sensory fibres of the shoulder girdle and upper limb.

It is formed by the **ventral rami** (the anterior branches of the spinal nerves) of **C5, C6, C7 and C8**, and nearly all of the ventral ramus of **T1**. A branch from C4 to C5 is seen occasionally, and a contribution from T2 is rare.

The limb buds appear in the 6th week of development. The anterior rami of the spinal nerves opposite the bases of the buds grow into the limbs. Because the muscle masses split into anterior and posterior groups, these rami become arranged in a plexus near the base of the limb.

The plexus is easy to learn if you remember the order **R, T, D, C, B**: **R**oots, **T**runks, **D**ivisions, **C**ords, **B**ranches.

## Formation: roots, trunks, divisions and cords

The roots enter the posterior triangle of the neck between **scalenus anterior and scalenus medius**. Then:

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/brachial-plexus/brachial-plexus-rtdcb-schematic.webp" alt="Schematic diagram of the brachial plexus showing roots, trunks, divisions, cords and terminal branches in order." caption="Roots, trunks, divisions, cords and branches: the plexus is easiest to learn as a schematic built on the theme of threes." credit="Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons" license="Public domain" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brachialplexusdetaileddiagram.jpg" width="960" height="742" /%}

- **Roots C5 and C6** join to form the **upper trunk**.
- **Root C7** continues as the **middle trunk**.
- **Roots C8 and T1** join to form the **lower trunk**.

Each of the three trunks divides into a **ventral (anterior) division** and a **dorsal (posterior) division**. These supply the anterior and posterior aspects of the limb. The divisions then join to form the cords.

{% table %}
- **Cord**
- **Formed by**
- **Mainly supplies**
---
- **Lateral cord**
- Ventral divisions of the upper and middle trunks (two divisions)
- Flexor (preaxial) muscles
---
- **Medial cord**
- Ventral division of the lower trunk (one division)
- Flexor (preaxial) muscles
---
- **Posterior cord**
- Dorsal divisions of all three trunks (three divisions)
- Extensor (postaxial) muscles
{% /table %}

The cords are **named by their relation to the second part of the axillary artery**. As a generalisation, the posterior cord supplies the extensor muscles, and the lateral and medial cords supply the flexor muscles.

The upper trunk divides 2 to 3 cm above the clavicle. The divisions of the middle and lower trunks form deep to the clavicle. The posterior division of the lower trunk is smaller than the anterior and is absent in about 10% of cases.

In a **postfixed plexus**, the contribution from T1 is large, T2 is always present, C4 is absent and C5 is reduced in size.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Draw it:** the best way to learn the plexus is to draw it again and again. Use the theme of threes: three trunks, three anterior and three posterior divisions, three cords. Label it from proximal to distal as roots, trunks, divisions, cords, branches.
{% /callout %}

## Position and relations

- **Roots and trunks** lie in the lower part of the posterior triangle of the neck, between the clavicle and the posteroinferior border of sternocleidomastoid. They are related behind to levator scapulae and scalenus medius, and in front to the prevertebral fascia, the internal jugular vein and sternocleidomastoid.
- **Divisions** lie behind the medial two-thirds of the clavicle, between the clavicle and the first rib.
- **Cords** lie in the axilla. They are the longest part of the plexus. In the lower axilla the plexus divides into its terminal branches.

The plexus is often split into a **supraclavicular part** (roots and trunks) and an **infraclavicular part** (divisions, cords and terminal branches). In other schemes the divisions are said to join the two parts.

The **axillary sheath** is formed from the prevertebral layer of deep cervical fascia. The subclavian artery and the plexus carry it with them from the interscalene triangle into the axilla. It encloses the plexus and the axillary artery.

The first and second parts of the axillary artery are related to the cords, and the third part is related to the branches. Around the artery, branches of the lateral cord lie lateral to it, branches of the medial cord lie medial to it and branches of the posterior cord lie behind it. The exception is the medial root of the median nerve. The formation and relations of the cords are variable, and the names can be somewhat misleading.

## Branches of the brachial plexus

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/brachial-plexus/brachial-plexus-branches-by-origin.webp" alt="Labelled diagram of the brachial plexus showing the named branches arising from roots, trunks and cords." caption="Branches arise from the roots, the upper trunk and the three cords, ending in five terminal nerves." credit="Brachial plexus.svg: Selket derivative work: mcstrother, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brachial_plexus_color.svg" width="960" height="394" /%}

{% table %}
- **Origin**
- **Branch (roots)**
- **Supplies**
---
- **Roots**
- Dorsal scapular (C5)
- Rhomboid major, rhomboid minor, levator scapulae
---
- **Roots**
- Long thoracic (C5, 6, 7)
- Serratus anterior
---
- **Upper trunk**
- Suprascapular (C5, 6)
- Supraspinatus and infraspinatus; also the shoulder joint
---
- **Upper trunk**
- Nerve to subclavius (C5, 6)
- Subclavius
---
- **Divisions**
- None
- The divisions give off no branches
---
- **Lateral cord**
- Lateral pectoral (C5, 6, 7)
- Pectoralis major, mainly its clavicular head
---
- **Lateral cord**
- Musculocutaneous (C5, 6, 7)
- Coracobrachialis, biceps brachii, brachialis; skin of the lateral forearm as the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm
---
- **Lateral cord**
- Lateral root of median (C5, 6, 7)
- Joins the medial root to form the median nerve
---
- **Medial cord**
- Medial pectoral (C8, T1)
- Pectoralis minor and pectoralis major
---
- **Medial cord**
- Medial cutaneous nerve of arm (C8, T1)
- Skin of a small area of the medial arm
---
- **Medial cord**
- Medial cutaneous nerve of forearm (C8, T1)
- Skin of a large area of the medial forearm
---
- **Medial cord**
- Ulnar (C7, 8, T1)
- C7 fibres arrive through a communicating branch from the lateral root of median
---
- **Medial cord**
- Medial root of median
- Crosses in front of the third part of the axillary artery to join the lateral root
---
- **Posterior cord**
- Upper subscapular (C5, 6)
- Upper part of subscapularis
---
- **Posterior cord**
- Thoracodorsal (C6, 7, 8)
- Latissimus dorsi
---
- **Posterior cord**
- Lower subscapular (C5, 6)
- Lower part of subscapularis and teres major
---
- **Posterior cord**
- Axillary
- Deltoid and teres minor; skin of the lower lateral shoulder
---
- **Posterior cord**
- Radial
- The largest branch of the plexus; triceps and the extensor compartment
{% /table %}

The posterior cord has consistent branches. In order they are the upper subscapular, thoracodorsal, lower subscapular, axillary and radial nerves.

The five **terminal branches** are the musculocutaneous, axillary, radial, median and ulnar nerves. Together they supply the arm, forearm and hand.

- **Musculocutaneous:** the nerve of the muscles of the front of the arm. It leaves the axilla by piercing coracobrachialis.
- **Axillary:** a terminal branch of the posterior cord. It turns deep and backward and passes through the quadrangular space.
- **Radial:** lies deep in the axilla, behind the axillary artery. It gives branches to the heads of triceps and the posterior cutaneous nerve of the arm, which supplies skin on the middle of the back of the arm.
- **Median:** formed by the union of the lateral and medial roots. It runs down on the lateral side of the axillary artery and gives off no branches in the axilla. It is the chief nerve of the muscles of the front of the forearm and of the thenar eminence.
- **Ulnar:** after the medial cord gives its medial root to the median nerve, it continues as the ulnar nerve.

Nerves that arise proximal to the terminal branches supply the shoulder muscles (long thoracic, suprascapular, subscapular nerves), the pectoral muscles (medial and lateral pectoral nerves) and the skin of the medial upper arm and forearm.

**Reflexes and roots:** the biceps reflex tests C5 and C6, the triceps reflex C6, C7 and C8, and the brachioradialis reflex C5, C6 and C7.

## Dermatomes of the upper limb

With the palm facing upward, trace around the arm: lateral upper arm (C5), lateral forearm (C6), thumb (C6), index and middle fingers (C7), ring and little fingers (C8), medial forearm (C8), medial upper arm (T1) and axilla (T2). The index finger may be supplied by C6 or C7.

## Blood supply of the plexus

The plexus is supplied by branches of the subclavian and vertebral arteries.

- Branches pass from the **vertebral artery** to the rami of C5 and C6 and the more proximal cervical nerves.
- Branches from the **costocervical trunk** give a rich supply to T1 and C8.
- The **thyrocervical trunk** gives the suprascapular and superficial cervical arteries (the transverse cervical artery in at least one-third of cases).

The thyrocervical trunk branches are the lifeline of the upper limb. They must be preserved in operations when a ruptured subclavian artery has not been repaired.

## Sympathetic fibres

The sympathetic supply of the upper limb comes from the upper thoracic spinal segments. Preganglionic fibres leave through the ventral roots and white rami and ascend the sympathetic chain to relay. The postganglionic fibres leave the middle cervical, inferior cervical and first thoracic (stellate) ganglia and join the roots of the plexus through grey rami. C5 and C6 receive grey rami from the middle cervical ganglion, and C7 and C8 receive them from the inferior cervical ganglion. These fibres travel with the peripheral nerves, and the supply is particularly rich in the palmar skin of the hand and the skin of the axilla.

Interruption of the white ramus from T1 is one cause of **Horner's syndrome**: drooping eyelid, narrow palpebral fissure, contracted pupil, and loss of sweating and vasomotor control on the same side of the face and neck.

## Applied anatomy: injuries (AN10.6)

The roots, trunks and divisions lie in the neck and the cords lie in the axilla, so a site of injury predicts the pattern of weakness. Complete lesions of all the roots are rare. Incomplete injuries are common and are usually caused by traction or pressure, and stab wounds can divide individual nerves.

{% table %}
- **Lesion**
- **Roots and site**
- **Features**
---
- **Erb's palsy (upper plexus)**
- C5 and C6 (sometimes C7); upper trunk, at Erb's point
- Biceps brachii, deltoid, brachialis and brachioradialis paralysed; supraspinatus, infraspinatus and supinator partly. The arm hangs by the side, adducted and medially rotated, with the forearm extended and pronated (waiter's tip or porter's tip posture). Sensation is lost over the lower half of the deltoid and the lateral forearm.
---
- **Klumpke's palsy (lower plexus)**
- C8 and T1; lower trunk
- Paralysis of the hand with the upper arm spared. Wrist and finger flexors are weak and the intrinsic hand muscles are paralysed. Sensation is lost in the ulnar forearm and hand. Horner's syndrome may be present.
---
- **Total plexus injury**
- All roots
- The whole limb is paralysed and numb. In adults it is usually caused by vehicle trauma, especially motorcycle injury with extreme hyperextension of the shoulder, causing multiple root avulsion.
{% /table %}

**Erb's point** is a region of the upper trunk where six nerves meet: the ventral rami of C5 and C6, the suprascapular nerve, the nerve to subclavius, and the anterior and posterior divisions of the upper trunk. Injury here causes Erb's paralysis.

**Causes of Erb's palsy:** undue separation of the head from the shoulder. This happens in difficult childbirth, in a fall on the shoulder and during anaesthesia. Stretch injuries at childbirth usually involve the upper plexus (Erb's palsy), much less often the lower plexus (Klumpke's palsy) or the entire plexus.

**Why the waiter's tip posture?** The C5 and C6 fibres in the suprascapular, musculocutaneous and axillary nerves are lost. The limb hangs medially rotated because the sternocostal part of pectoralis major is unopposed. It is adducted because supraspinatus and deltoid are lost. The forearm is pronated because biceps is lost.

**Mnemonic for Erb and Klumpke:** E and D come alphabetically before K, and Erb-Duchenne affects roots that are higher (C5, C6) than Klumpke (C8, T1).

**Clinical problem:** a patient cannot abduct the shoulder, flex the elbow or supinate the forearm. The lesion is at Erb's point in the upper trunk, so the deltoid, biceps, brachialis and supinator are paralysed.

**Birth injury:** after a difficult delivery the baby has a floppy or flail arm. In Erb's palsy the baby's arm is held to the side, internally rotated and pronated. In Klumpke's palsy the arm is supinated with the elbow flexed and the intrinsic hand muscles are weak. In a total plexus injury the arm is flail and pale, all finger muscles are paralysed, and there may be Horner's syndrome. Sensation cannot be tested in a baby.

**Other causes of plexus injury:**

- Traction from violent shoulder movements in falls, shoulder dislocation and heavy packs on the shoulder (**rucksack paralysis**)
- Penetrating injuries to the axilla and surgical injury, such as axillary dissection and subclavian puncture
- A forceful, transient blow to the shoulder in football (**stinger or burner syndrome**)
- Tumours, especially breast and lung, and radiation plexopathy, which appears after a delay of months
- Neuralgic amyotrophy (brachial plexitis): sudden pain in the shoulder and upper arm, then weakness and wasting

## Preganglionic and postganglionic lesions

This distinction decides whether surgery can help.

- **Preganglionic lesion (root avulsion):** the root is torn from the spinal cord, proximal to the dorsal root ganglion. It cannot recover and is surgically irreparable.
- **Postganglionic lesion:** rupture of a root distal to the ganglion, or of a trunk or peripheral nerve. It can recover or may be repairable.

Lesions in continuity generally have a better prognosis than complete ruptures. Mild lesions (neurapraxia) can follow trivial trauma, such as a tight harness or seatbelt, and recover spontaneously.

**Features suggesting root avulsion:**

1. Crushing or burning pain in an anaesthetic hand
2. Paralysis of the scapular muscles or diaphragm
3. Horner's syndrome (ptosis, miosis, enophthalmos and anhidrosis)
4. Severe vascular injury
5. Associated fractures of the cervical spine
6. Spinal cord dysfunction, for example hyper-reflexia in the lower limbs

**Localising the lesion:** examine each component in turn. Suppose the dorsal scapular nerve (rhomboids), long thoracic nerve (serratus anterior) and suprascapular nerve (supraspinatus) work, but the musculocutaneous (biceps), radial (triceps) and axillary (deltoid) nerves do not. This suggests a lateral and posterior cord injury.

**Vessels:** brachial plexus injuries are often high-energy and may come with life-threatening trauma. Look for rupture of the subclavian or axillary artery. The second part of the axillary artery, behind pectoralis minor, is most at risk of occlusion in infraclavicular traction injuries to the plexus.

## Thoracic outlet syndrome

The plexus, the subclavian artery and the subclavian vein lie close to the upper surface of the first rib and the clavicle as they enter the upper limb. Narrowing here can compress them. Most symptoms come from pressure on the **lower trunk**: pain down the medial side of the forearm and hand, and wasting of the small muscles of the hand. Pressure on the vessels may impair circulation in the limb.

Common causes are an incomplete cervical rib with a fibrous band to the first rib, a taut band from an elongated C7 transverse process to the first rib, a complete cervical rib, and anomalies of the scalene muscles. Neurogenic thoracic outlet syndrome is rare, and the diagnosis is made too often. It is commonly confused with carpal tunnel syndrome, ulnar neuropathy and cervical radiculopathy.

## Injuries to terminal nerves

- **Radial nerve lesion:** wrist drop.
- **Axillary nerve lesion:** contributes to the waiter's tip posture.
- **Median nerve lesion:** the thenar muscles are paralysed and wasted, and the thumb cannot oppose. The hand looks flattened, called "ape hand".

## Brachial plexus block in the axilla

The axillary sheath encloses the plexus and the axillary artery, so local anaesthetic placed around the artery can block the nerves of the arm. In the axilla the median and musculocutaneous nerves lie above the artery, and the ulnar and radial nerves lie below and behind it. With ultrasound, the median nerve is usually between the lateral aspect of the artery and biceps, the ulnar nerve is more superficial and medial to the artery, and the radial nerve is deep to the artery. The relations vary a great deal between people, and the ultrasound-guided axillary approach is a multi-injection technique. Tourniquet pain comes from the intercostobrachial nerve and is blocked with a subcutaneous injection in the axilla.

## Mnemonics

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Draw the M:** the lateral and medial cords and their branches form a giant capital **M**. The **M**usculocutaneous and **U**lnar nerves are the sides, and the **M**edian nerve is the middle.
{% /callout %}

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Lateral to medial around the axillary artery:** **M**usculocutaneous, **A**xillary, **R**adial, **M**edian, **U**lnar (MARMU).
{% /callout %}

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Pectoral nerves:** "**L**ateral **L**ess, **M**edial **M**ore". The medial pectoral nerve supplies both pectoralis major and minor. **C5 to C7** raise your arms up to heaven: the long thoracic nerve (C5 to C7) supplies serratus anterior, and the ability to raise the arm past 90 degrees tests these roots.
{% /callout %}

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The medial cord is the continuation of the ventral division of the lower trunk. The lateral cord comes from the ventral divisions of the upper and middle trunks, and the posterior cord comes from the dorsal divisions of all three trunks." %}
The medial cord of the brachial plexus is formed by:
- A. The dorsal divisions of all three trunks
- B. The ventral division of the lower trunk
- C. The ventral divisions of the upper and middle trunks
- D. The ventral division of the middle trunk only
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="The musculocutaneous nerve arises from the lateral cord. The upper subscapular, thoracodorsal, lower subscapular, axillary and radial nerves are the branches of the posterior cord." %}
Which of the following is NOT a branch of the posterior cord?
- A. Axillary nerve
- B. Radial nerve
- C. Thoracodorsal nerve
- D. Musculocutaneous nerve
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The suprascapular nerve (C5, C6) arises from the upper trunk and supplies supraspinatus and infraspinatus. The divisions of the plexus give off no branches." %}
The suprascapular nerve arises from the:
- A. Lateral cord
- B. Posterior cord
- C. Upper trunk
- D. Anterior division of the lower trunk
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="Inability to abduct the shoulder, flex the elbow and supinate the forearm points to loss of C5 and C6 fibres through the upper trunk, at Erb's point. This is Erb's paralysis. Klumpke's palsy affects C8 and T1 and gives paralysis of the hand." %}
A patient cannot abduct the shoulder, flex the elbow or supinate the forearm. The most likely site of injury is:
- A. The upper trunk at Erb's point (C5, C6)
- B. The lower trunk (C8, T1)
- C. The posterior cord
- D. The medial cord
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Avulsion of a root from the spinal cord is a preganglionic lesion, proximal to the dorsal root ganglion. It cannot recover and is surgically irreparable. Postganglionic lesions can recover or be repaired." %}
Which statement about a preganglionic brachial plexus lesion is correct?
- A. It lies distal to the dorsal root ganglion
- B. It is proximal to the dorsal root ganglion and is surgically irreparable
- C. It usually recovers fully without treatment
- D. It is the same as a rupture of a trunk
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="In thoracic outlet syndrome most symptoms come from pressure on the lower trunk. This causes pain down the medial side of the forearm and hand and wasting of the small muscles of the hand." %}
Pressure on which part of the plexus causes most of the symptoms of thoracic outlet syndrome?
- A. The upper trunk, causing weakness of the shoulder abductors
- B. The posterior cord, causing wrist drop
- C. The lower trunk, causing pain in the medial forearm and wasting of the hand muscles
- D. The roots C5 and C6, causing loss of the biceps reflex
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="Which nerves form the brachial plexus?" %}
The ventral rami of C5, C6, C7 and C8 and nearly all of the ventral ramus of T1.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="How are the trunks formed?" %}
C5 and C6 form the upper trunk, C7 continues as the middle trunk, and C8 and T1 form the lower trunk. They lie between scalenus anterior and scalenus medius.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="How are the cords formed and why are they so named?" %}
The lateral cord comes from the ventral divisions of the upper and middle trunks. The medial cord is the ventral division of the lower trunk. The posterior cord comes from the dorsal divisions of all three trunks. They are named by their relation to the second part of the axillary artery.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the branches of the posterior cord." %}
Upper subscapular, thoracodorsal, lower subscapular, axillary and radial nerves.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is Erb's point and what is Erb's paralysis?" %}
Erb's point is a region of the upper trunk where six nerves meet. Injury to the upper trunk (C5, C6) causes Erb's paralysis. The arm hangs adducted and medially rotated, with the forearm extended and pronated (waiter's tip posture).
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which roots are affected in Klumpke's palsy, and what is the result?" %}
C8 and T1, the lower plexus. The hand is paralysed with the upper arm spared. The wrist and finger flexors are weak, the intrinsic hand muscles are paralysed, and there may be Horner's syndrome.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is the difference between a preganglionic and a postganglionic lesion?" %}
A preganglionic lesion is a root avulsion from the spinal cord, proximal to the dorsal root ganglion. It cannot recover and is surgically irreparable. A postganglionic lesion is distal to the ganglion and can recover or be repaired.
{% /viva %}

